[DIAGNOSIS]:
The function leq is incorrectly declared with a return type Bool, but its body is a Boolean formula (not a set/relation or enum value); in Alloy, such functions must omit the return type so Alloy treats them as Boolean-valued predicates or helper functions.

[FIX INTENT]:
Remove the explicit return type Bool from the leq function so Alloy infers the Boolean result type.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Locate the function declaration fun leq[t1, t2: Time]: Bool { ... } at line 75.
- Step 2: Remove the : Bool return type, so the declaration reads fun leq[t1, t2: Time] { ... }.
- Step 3: Optionally, replace manual ordering logic in leq and lt with the built-in ordering predicates from util/ordering[Time] module, as suggested by the user guidance.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because Boolean-valued helper functions in Alloy must not declare an explicit enum or signature as a return type; omitting the return type allows Alloy to infer a PrimitiveBoolean result, matching the expected usage.

[LESSON]:
When writing Boolean helper functions in Alloy, never specify a return type; always let Alloy infer the type from the Boolean formula in the function body.
```

