Previous Lessons:
1. When writing helper functions in Alloy that return logical truth values, do not specify an enum or signature as the return type; let Alloy infer a PrimitiveBoolean result from the formula.
2. Alloy 6 requires Boolean-valued functions to specify their return type as Bool and use a single Boolean formula as the body; curly braces for function bodies require a return value, not a predicate/Boolean formula.
3. Alloy doesn't support method-like function application with arguments in all contexts; must use relational join and comprehension.
4. Alloy 6 requires all function declarations to have an explicit return type; omitting the return type causes a syntax error.
5. Can't return an enum value (e.g., Bool) from a function whose body is a Boolean formula in Alloy 6 → Must omit the return type and allow Alloy to infer a PrimitiveBoolean result for Boolean-valued functions.

